Actually, [38;5;10mcargo test[0m for integration tests compiles the crate as an [38;5;10mrlib[0m (not cdylib) and statically links it into the test binary. The cdylib is built separately. So the test binary has the Rust code statically linked, and also loads both .so files via libloading.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
But the C library's internal function calls go through the PLT. When the C .so is loaded, its PLT entries for [38;5;10mc2GJK[0m, [38;5;10mc2AABBtoCapsuleManifold[0m, etc. might resolve to the Rust versions that are in the test binary's symbol table (since the test binary has them statically linked and they might be in the global symbol table).[0m[0m
